Demystifying On-Policy Distillation:Where Does the Teacher’s Advantage Live? Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.19145v1 [cs.LG] 13 Oct 2026 Demystifying On-Policy Distillation: Where Does the Teacher’s Advantage Live? Yiping Li Zhibo Hou Fan Zhao Zhiyu An Wan Du Affiliation: University of California, Merced, USA Email: {yipingli,zhou6,fanzhao,zan7,wdu3}@ucmerced.edu Abstract On-policy distillation (OPD) is increasingly studied as a cheaper alternative to reinforcement learning, yet it remains a form of knowledge distillation (KD) that relies on teacher supervision. We revisit OPD from the KD perspective by asking where the teacher’s advantage lies along student-generated trajectories. Controlled token flips at teacher–student disagreements directly measure the accuracy gained from teacher corrections. Single-token flips yield a modest average gain on selected failed trajectories, whereas repeated flips raise accuracy from 28.4% to 74.5%, close to the teacher’s 76.9%. Gains continue to accumulate with more flips, but mass- and KL-based selection outperform random flips by 15–18 percentage points at counts of similar magnitude. Selective intervention remains useful after OPD, but greater argmax agreement does not consistently improve accuracy across model pairs. We advocate training schedules that learn more valuable teacher corrections while maintaining distributional stability. Controlled flips and fork-resolution analysis evaluate OPD beyond accuracy by measuring which teacher preferences the student acquires and what further correction can recover. 1 Introduction Reinforcement learning improves large language models after pretraining, but it is expensive ( DeepSeek-AI, 2025 ; Yang et al., 2025 ; Yu et al., 2025 ; Chen et al., 2025 ) . On-policy distillation (OPD) offers a cheaper route by training the student on its own rollouts toward a teacher’s next-token distribution ( Agarwal et al., 2024 ; Lu and Thinking Machines Lab, 2025 ) . Reported results reach RL-level accuracy at about a tenth of the cost ( Yang et al., 2025 ; Lu and Thinking Machines Lab, 2025 ) . This efficiency has brought OPD into frontier post-training pipelines and attracted research interest ( DeepSeek-AI, 2026 ; Xiaomi LLM-Core Team, 2026 ; GLM-5-Team, 2026 ; Kimi Team, 2026 ; Yang et al., 2026b ; Song and Zheng, 2026 ) . Yet OPD is commonly treated as an RL method because it serves as a cheaper substitute for RL in post-training. This view captures its role in the pipeline, but OPD still depends on a teacher and remains a form of knowledge distillation (KD). In conventional KD, soft labels preserve similarities that hard labels discard ( Hinton et al., 2015 ) . For OPD, however, it remains unclear what in the teacher’s next-token distributions contributes to better reasoning and where that contribution appears along the student’s trajectory. Prior work suggests that distillation quality depends on both what the teacher provides and what the student can learn ( Li et al., 2022 ) . Therefore, OPD training outcomes alone do not distinguish the teacher’s available advantage from the student’s ability to acquire it. We first examine the teacher’s contribution through interventions that hold the student’s parameters fixed, then analyze how OPD checkpoints change the disagreement. In this paper, we focus on forks , positions at which the student and teacher diverge in their next-token preferences. We measure the teacher’s advantage over the student through single and repeated token flips. A single flip replaces the student’s token at one fork and lets the student continue. Dual-track decoding repeats this one-token intervention at selected forks as the trajectory develops. We use these interventions to ask how much accuracy teacher corrections recover and whether selecting particular forks improves that recovery. Checkpoint analysis then asks which preferences OPD acquires and whether learning them produces better answers. Our contributions are the following. • We develop an intervention-based evaluation of OPD. Controlled flips measure the accuracy recoverable through teacher corrections, while checkpoint analysis measures which teacher preferences the student acquires. This separates the value of supervision from its learnability. • We characterize how teacher corrections improve student trajectories. Gains accumulate through repeated flips, but mass- and KL-based selection recover more accuracy than random flips at intervention counts of similar magnitude. Teacher guidance is therefore broadly useful without making all disagreements equally valuable. • We identify a mismatch between token-level learning and trajectory-level improvement. Across model pairs, fork resolution can increase while accuracy stagnates or declines, and selective intervention remains useful after training. Resolving disagreements alone cannot establish whether OPD turns teacher guidance into better answers. 2 Related Work On-policy distillation and its variants. OPD trains on student-generated trajectories with teacher supervision, often using reverse KL ( Agarwal et al., 2024 ; Lu and Thinking Machines Lab, 2025 ) . TA-OPD retains high-teachability tokens, with a 10% budget in its main experiments ( Wang et al., 2026c ) , while TIP emphasizes low-entropy tokens with high teacher–student divergence ( Xu et al., 2026b ) . Prefix methods study shorter rollouts and supervision from early trajectory segments ( Zhang et al., 2026a ; Zhou et al., 2026 ; Liu et al., 2026a ; Zhang et al., 2026b ) . Other methods modify supervision through advantage regulation or baselines ( Wang et al., 2026b ; Zhao et al., 2026 ; Oh et al., 2026 ) , support-aware matching, asymmetric objectives, or reward extrapolation ( Fu et al., 2026 ; Jia et al., 2026 ; Yang et al., 2026a ) . EOPD adds forward KL at high-teacher-entropy positions ( Jin et al., 2026 ) , while TrOPD chooses the divergence using a trust region on the teacher-to-student ratio ( Xing et al., 2026 ) . TGPO supervises teacher-preferred tokens on student-generated prefixes without intervening in generation ( Liu et al., 2026c ) . Teacher intervention instead changes the rollout through reflection-triggered handoffs ( Xu et al., 2026a ) , backtracking or prefix refinement ( Wang et al., 2026a ; Jiang et al., 2026 ) , or trust-region behavior blending during warm-up ( Plyusov et al., 2026 ) . Other work changes the data or reference ( Liu et al., 2026b ; Luo et al., 2026 ; Hou et al., 2026 ) , and Song and Zheng (2026) survey OPD. These rules encode different assumptions about where the teacher’s advantage lies. They favor sparse positions, uncertain positions, prefixes, or reflection words, but they do not directly test whether the advantage is localized in any of them. We instead ask where the teacher’s higher trajectory-level accuracy enters the student’s trajectory. Analyses of OPD. A smaller line of work studies OPD rather than extending it. Li et al. (2026) examine which teachers help which students by tracking how their distributions move during training. Wang et al. (2026b) study the role and failure modes of OPD through training curves across teachers and benchmarks. Luo et al. (2026) connect instability to the dynamics of the per-token advantage. These analyses take the training run as their object, so the outcome combines what the teacher offers with what the student learns. Our interventions isolate the teacher’s contribution with fixed student parameters, and checkpoint comparisons separately track changes in token-level disagreement. Assisted and guided decoding. Combining models during decoding has been used both as a method and as a lens for analysis. Speculative decoding lets a large model accept or replace tokens drafted by a smaller model, with speed as the objective ( Leviathan et al., 2023 ; Chen et al., 2023 ) . Contrastive decoding and proxy tuning combine model predictions to improve the generated sequence ( Li et al., 2023 ; Liu et al., 2024 ) . We use a lockstep teacher–student pairing as a measurement in which the student retains its sampled token unless the intervention selects the position. The number and location of teacher substitutions then become the quantities of interest. 3 Preliminaries On-policy distillation. Let π S \pi_{S} be the student and π T \pi_{T} the teacher. Given a prompt P P , OPD trains on the student’s own trajectories x = ( x 1 , … , x T ) ∼ π S ( ⋅ ∣ P ) x=(x_{1},\dots,x_{T})\sim\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid P) of length T T . At every position t t the two models see the same prefix and propose a next token: q t = π S ( ⋅ ∣ P , x < t ) q_{t}=\pi_{S}(\cdot\mid P,x_{<t}) is the student’s distribution and p t = π T ( ⋅ ∣ P , x < t ) p_{t}=\pi_{T}(\cdot\mid P,x_{<t}) the teacher’s. The objective pays, at every position, a weighted price for being different from the teacher: ℒ = 𝔼 x ∼ π S ∑ t = 1 T w t KL t , KL t = KL ( q t ∥ p t ) . \mathcal{L}=\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\pi_{S}}\!\sum_{t=1}^{T}w_{t}\mathrm{KL}_{t},\quad\mathrm{KL}_{t}=\mathrm{KL}(q_{t}\|p_{t}). (1) where w t w_{t} is the weight of position t t , with w t ≡ 1 w_{t}\equiv 1 for uniform supervision. Summing over the whole vocabulary at every position is expensive, so our top- k k approximation keeps the tokens the student ranks highest, with k = 16 k=16 : KL t ≈ KL ^ t = ∑ x ∈ Top k ​ ( q t ) q t ​ ( x ) ​ log ⁡ q t ​ ( x ) p t ​ ( x ) . \mathrm{KL}_{t}\approx\widehat{\mathrm{KL}}_{t}=\!\!\sum_{x\in\mathrm{Top}_{k}(q_{t})}\!\!q_{t}(x)\log rac{q_{t}(x)}{p_{t}(x)}. (2) The gradient therefore reaches only tokens the student already considers plausible; a teacher token outside Top k ​ ( q t ) \mathrm{Top}_{k}(q_{t}) is never pushed up directly. Choice of divergence. Equation 1 uses the reverse KL, which is mode-seeking: the student is charged for probability it places where the teacher places little. GKD also considers forward KL and Jensen–Shannon ( Agarwal et al., 2024 ) , while TrOPD uses a position-dependent mix of reverse and forward KL ( Xing et al., 2026 ) . Our experiments use reverse KL throughout. 4 Setup 4.1 Models, Data, and Grading The student π S \pi_{S} is Qwen3-1.7B in non-thinking mode; the teacher π T \pi_{T} is Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 ( Yang et al., 2025 ) . They share a tokenizer, so tokens compare position by position. Problems come from DAPO-Math-17k ( Yu et al., 2025 ) . A trajectory is correct when its final Answer: /  oxed expression is equivalent to the reference. Unless stated otherwise both models sample at temperature 0.7 0.7 , top- p p 0.95 0.95 , and teacher-alone baselines use the same sampling. All runs use a fixed seed of 0; the hardware, software and decoding settings of every stage are listed in App. A . 4.2 Fork Definitions and Scores Fork. A fork is a position where the student and teacher prefer different next tokens under the same prefix, arg ⁡ max ⁡ q t ≠ arg ⁡ max ⁡ p t , \arg\max q_{t}
eq\arg\max p_{t}, (3) while the strength of their disagreement is measured separately below. A sampled student token can still equal the teacher’s argmax at a fork, so a fork need not produce a token replacement. Token mass. KL measures distribution-level disagreement but is unbounded and is not a metric ( Cover and Thomas, 2006 ) . We also use token mass to distinguish two directions of disagreement: the teacher assigning little probability to the student’s token, and the student assigning little probability to the teacher’s choice. For a token set S S , these probabilities are μ t T ​ ( S ) = ∑ x ∈ S p t ​ ( x ) , μ t S ​ ( S ) = ∑ x ∈ S q t ​ ( x ) , \mu^{T}_{t}(S)=\sum_{x\in S}p_{t}(x),\qquad\mu^{S}_{t}(S)=\sum_{x\in S}q_{t}(x), (4) and S S can be a single token or a top- k k set, for example { x t } \{x_{t}\} or Top k ​ ( q t ) \mathrm{Top}_{k}(q_{t}) . Similar quantities appear in prior work ( Xing et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026a ; Wang et al., 2026c ) . Flip. A flip replaces the student’s token x t x_{t} at a fork with a teacher token, either sampled from p t p_{t} or chosen as its argmax. Each experiment specifies the replacement rule and continuation model. Changing one token does not make the subsequent continuation a teacher-generated trajectory. Blind Forks. A fork is blind when the teacher’s preferred token lies outside the student’s top- k k candidates, arg ⁡ max ⁡ p t ∉ Top k ​ ( q t ) \arg\max p_{t}
