otin\mathrm{Top}_{k}(p_{t}) . Reflection words. We use the 13-word list of Relay-OPD ( Xu et al., 2026a ) , including wait , but , and actually , matched at the token level ( Section A.4.1 ). This operational definition includes words that can also occur outside reflection. Table 1: Single-flip gains over resampling and net-gain differences versus remaining positions, in pp. Each estimate is paired with its test at the same aggregation level. Share uses 5,901 eligible forks. Group tests are unadjusted. Full results and eligibility rules appear in Tables 7 and B.1 . Flip − - Resample Net Gain − - Rest Trajectory Problem Trajectory Problem Fork Group Share (%) Gain p T p_{T} Gain p P p_{P} Δ \Delta p T p_{T} Δ \Delta p P p_{P} All Forks 100.0 +1.41 ∗∗∗ < 0.001 <0.001 +1.22 ∗ 0.015 – – – – μ t S < 0.05 \mu_{t}^{S}<0.05 53.9 +2.06 ∗∗∗ < 0.001 <0.001 +1.83 ∗∗ 0.007 +1.40 ∗∗∗ < 0.001 <0.001 +1.35 ∗∗ 0.004 Blind 9.0 +5.27 ∗∗∗ < 0.001 <0.001 +4.27 ∗ 0.012 +4.08 ∗∗∗ < 0.001 <0.001 +3.31 ∗ 0.015 Reflection/Contrast 6.6 +6.59 ∗ 0.045 +5.11 0.116 +1.84 ∗∗ 0.008 +3.84 0.189 4.3 Interventions Single flip. One fork of an existing student trajectory is flipped and nothing else before it is changed. The teacher supplies a sampled segment of L L tokens, including the first replacement, and the student then writes the rest of the trajectory. At L = ∞ L=\infty the teacher finishes the trajectory. The baseline independently resamples the student from the same prefix, including the fork token, to measure gain over another student continuation. Dual-track decoding. We use dual-track decoding as a controlled intervention on student generation. Both models condition on the current prefix, with one forward pass each per step. At each fork selected by the flip condition, we replace only the student’s next token with the teacher’s argmax, arg ⁡ max ⁡ p t \arg\max p_{t} , then return control to the student. At all other positions, the student samples from its own distribution. Replacements stop once an answer marker appears. We also test sampling the replacement from the teacher’s distribution while keeping the same selection rule ( Table 15 ). This measures the combined effect of repeated token replacements along the resulting trajectory. 5 Empirical Analysis We sample four trajectories per problem from the student before OPD training, using 1,000 DAPO-Math problems. The student and teacher each score the same 5.24M token positions to characterize their disagreement. The interventions then use subsets of these problems, specified below. 1 1 1 Throughout the paper, asterisks indicate statistical significance: ∗ p < 0.05 {}^{*}p<0.05 , p ∗ ⁣ ∗ < 0.01 {}^{**}p<0.01 , and ∗ ∗ ∗ p < 0.001 {}^{***}p<0.001 . Test details and units of analysis are given in App. B . 5.1 Population Statistics The student and teacher prefer the same next token at most positions, with forks accounting for 13.93% of the rollout. The top 20% of positions ranked by reverse KL contribute 94% of the loss ( App. C ). At about 63% of profiled forks, the teacher’s preferred token is the student’s second or third choice ( App. C ). We test whether correcting disagreements improves final answers rather than assuming that loss concentration identifies useful corrections. 5.2 Single Flip We first ask whether one teacher token can rescue a failed trajectory. We select 10 problems that the teacher solves 4/4 from the prompt and the student 0/4 to make the performance gap observable. We intervene at 5,926 forks across 40 failed trajectories and compare recovery with 16 independent student resamples per prefix. Single-token estimates retain changed-token samples at 5,901 forks; longer segments and continuation counts are specified in Section A.3 . 5.2.1 How Much Does a Single Teacher Token Help? Single-token replacement improves success from 9.91% to 11.31%, a trajectory-weighted gain of 1.41 pp over resampling at the same position (95% CI [ 0.66 , 2.15 ] [0.66,2.15] , p T < 0.001 p_{T}<0.001 ). The problem-weighted gain is 1.22 pp ( p P = 0.015 p_{P}=0.015 , Table 1 ). Extending the teacher’s segment increases the trajectory-weighted mean gain to 3.25 pp at L = 16 L=16 , 16.51 at L = 128 L=128 , and 79.68 when the teacher writes to the end ( Section D.1 ). Whether the inserted token is the teacher’s argmax or a lower-ranked sample makes no detectable difference in the original A100 continuation runs ( p = 0.27 p=0.27 ). The gain also holds against a keep-token baseline that preserves the original student token and resamples only its continuation ( Section D.5 ). 5.2.2 Which Forks Benefit More from a Single Replacement? We also report reflection/contrast positions because they may mark a change in reasoning direction, defining them by the teacher’s preferred next token. The average gain varies across fork groups ( Table 1 ). Positions with μ t S < 0.05 \mu_{t}^{S}<0.05 and blind positions show larger gains than their complements, by 1.35 and 3.31 pp respectively under problem-level weighting, with both comparisons significant. The larger gain at blind forks mainly reflects lower student-resampling success rather than much higher replacement success ( Section D.3 ). Reflection/contrast positions have a positive estimated gain over resampling, but neither this gain nor its difference from other forks is significant under the problem-level test. Full group comparisons across intervention lengths are reported in Section D.2 . Despite these differences in average gain, the tested features predict only weakly whether an individual flip outperforms resampling. Combining all features yields a cross-validated AUC of 0.554 ( Section D.4 ). Group averages can favor a selection rule even when the features do not reliably distinguish which flips outperform resampling. 5.2.3 Does Intervention Timing Matter? Later prefixes are harder to recover even when the teacher completes the continuation ( App. E ). Paired single-flip gains do not distinguish a position group from the remaining forks under both aggregation levels. Timing primarily describes recoverability rather than incremental token value. 5.2.4 Can the Student Follow a Teacher Correction? A correction could lead into a continuation the student struggles to follow, but entropy approaches the unchanged-token control after about 40 tokens ( Section F.2 ). On teacher trajectories, 73.4% of fork runs contain only one fork and fewer than 1% reach five tokens ( Section F.1 ), suggesting local rather than sustained disagreement. 5.3 Dual-Track Decoding We apply repeated flips on 200 randomly sampled problems with four trajectories each. Since flips can shorten or extend trajectories, we compare rules at flip counts of similar magnitude. Figure 1: Accuracy versus mean flips per trajectory. Point labels indicate selection thresholds. Full results are in Table 13 . 5.3.1 How Much Do Repeated Flips Improve Accuracy? The teacher’s contribution accumulates over repeated corrections rather than requiring the student to follow an entire teacher-generated trajectory ( Figure 1 ). From a student baseline of 28.4%, random flips raise accuracy to 36.1% with about 23 flips per trajectory, 52.0% with 103 flips, and 57.3% with 157 flips. At about 408 flips, covering roughly 90% of forks, accuracy reaches 74.5%, compared with the teacher’s 76.9%. To check whether late teacher hints drive the gains, we stop intervention earlier and let the student finish alone. Gains remain roughly proportional to the preceding flips across exit positions ( Section G.2 ), supporting accumulated correction rather than late answer hints as an explanation for the improvement. 5.3.2 Does Fork Selection Matter? Both μ t T \mu_{t}^{T} - and KL-based selection shift the accuracy curve upward relative to random flips ( Figure 1 ). They achieve higher accuracy with flip counts of similar magnitude. For example, at about 23–26 flips per trajectory, the two rules outperform random selection by 15.4 and 18.1 pp, respectively (both p < 10 − 9 p<10^{-9} ). The KL curve is slightly higher than the μ t T \mu_{t}^{T} curve over much of the tested range. Mass and KL are therefore useful for allocating repeated corrections despite their weak prediction of individual single-flip gains. Ablation experiments across model sizes, model families, and datasets show similar patterns ( Sections G.3 to G.5 ). 5.3.3 Do Reflection Tokens Provide a Particular Advantage? Reflection-based selection increases the student’s subsequent reflection-word use and trajectory length, but does not significantly improve accuracy over random flips ( p = 0.29 p=0.29 , Table 2 ). In contrast, μ t T < 10 − 5 \mu_{t}^{T}<10^{-5} achieves significantly higher accuracy than random flips with fewer interventions, without a comparable increase in reflection-word use. Its accuracy advantage over reflection-based selection is not significant ( p = 0.07 p=0.07 ). More visible reflection is therefore not sufficient evidence of more effective teacher guidance. Table 2: Reflection-word use, trajectory length, and accuracy under sparse intervention. Reflection counts exclude teacher-inserted tokens. Significance compares accuracy with random flips. Flipped Flips Introduced Reflection Words Length Accuracy (%) Student alone 0 2.5 1,345 28.4 random 6% 11 3.2 1,506 32.9 reflection words 14 8.4 2,156 34.9 μ t T < 10 − 5 \mu^{T}_{t}<10^{-5} ∗∗ 4 3.1 1,594 38.4 5.3.4 Can Teacher Intervention Hurt the Student? Teacher intervention need not help on every problem, even when the teacher performs better overall. On problems the student solves but the teacher does not, intervention reduces student accuracy from 31.2% to 18.8% in a small subgroup of 16 trajectories ( Section G.6 ). This shows that teacher corrections can also move the student away from a correct answer. Sampled and argmax teacher replacements are compared in Section G.7 . 5.4 After OPD Having examined where the teacher’s advantage lies, we next ask how much of it the student acquires through vanilla OPD. The main analysis uses Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 as teacher and Qwen3-1.7B as student on DAPO-Math. We extend checkpoint analysis to the 4B-to-0.6B and 8B-to-4B Qwen pairs, Llama 3.2 3B-to-1B, and Gemma 3 12B-to-4B. We also examine an instruction-following run of the 1.7B Qwen student evaluated on IFEval ( Section A.6.5 ). Figure 2: Fork changes on pre-training prefixes (left, % of original forks) and accuracy on each checkpoint’s own rollouts (right). 5.4.1 How Does Fork Resolution Change During Training? We track fork changes by scoring the same pre-training student trajectories with each checkpoint. • Solved. An original fork now agrees with the teacher. • Unchanged. An original fork retains the pre-training student’s preference. • Moved. An original fork chooses neither the teacher’s nor the pre-training student’s token. • New. A position where the pre-training student and teacher agreed now disagrees with the teacher. Fork-group proportions generally change smoothly during training, with few large reversals across model pairs. Solved forks increase and Unchanged forks decrease. In the main Qwen pair, Solved and New proportions settle near 35.5% and 12.9% of the original fork count by Final ( Figures 2 and 16 ). The plateau may reflect limited capacity to acquire further teacher preferences. However, accuracy does not consistently improve as more forks are resolved and can fluctuate while token agreement improves steadily. This suggests that OPD’s effect on final answers depends on changes beyond agreement with the teacher at fork positions ( Section H.1.2 ). 5.4.2 Which Forks Are More Learnable? Figure 3: Fork resolution by pre-training teacher mass and reverse KL. Colors show within-group rates, right-hand values show final rates, and parentheses show shares of original forks. Fork resolution exhibits distinct mass- and KL-based patterns across model pairs rather than a universal ordering ( Figures 3 and H.1.4 ). Runs using the same Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 teacher show similar patterns despite changes in student size or dataset ( Section H.1.4 ). These patterns suggest that the teacher shapes which token choices are easier to learn, while the amount acquired varies with the student and training setting. Blind forks are less often resolved despite their larger average single-flip gains. In the main Qwen pair, their resolution rate is 14.6%, compared with 37.0% for non-blind forks, consistent with the top-16 loss lacking an explicit supervision term for the teacher’s preferred token. 5.4.3 How Much Does Teacher Intervention Help after OPD? Figure 4: Mass selection and random flips at three OPD checkpoints. Labels give mass thresholds or random probabilities. Gray lines mark standalone accuracy. Each point uses 800 trajectories. Mass-based selection remains more effective than random flips at low intervention counts after OPD ( Figure 4 ). Its curves rise steeply and then flatten, while random flips require more interventions to reach similar accuracy. Applying the same mass threshold brings checkpoints with different starting accuracies to similar performance, suggesting that training has not exhausted the useful teacher corrections identified by this rule. 5.4.4 Which Remaining Forks Are Useful Intervention Targets? Figure 5: Entropy on fixed pre-training prefixes (purple, left axes) and accuracy on checkpoint rollouts (gray, right axes). Teacher entropy is dotted. Post-answer repetition is excluded. All six settings appear in Section H.4 . We ask whether intervention is more useful at disagreements that persist through training or those introduced by training. At each fork encountered during generation, we also check the pre-training student’s preferred token. If it agrees with the teacher, the fork is New. Otherwise, the fork is Unchanged if the trained student retains its original preference, or Moved if it chooses a different token. We compare these group-based interventions with random flips at counts of similar magnitude. Prioritizing New or Moved forks does not produce a consistent accuracy advantage across model pairs ( Section H.3 ). In the main Qwen pair, New selection underperforms random controls, while Moved offers no detectable advantage. In Gemma and Llama, neither group significantly improves accuracy over unassisted decoding. Unchanged forks still provide useful corrections, but do not outperform random flips in the Qwen comparisons ( Table 3 ). Newly introduced or changed preferences are therefore weak signals of where further teacher correction will help. Table 3: Fork-group interventions at step 16. F/T is flips per trajectory and F/F is the percentage of forks flipped. p p compares accuracy with Random in each block ( Section B.2 ). Fork Group F/T F/F Acc. (%) p p Random 96.6 20.4 57.13 – Old 100.0 19.7 56.75 0.837 Unchanged 94.5 18.5 57.63 0.761 Random 28.9 5.9 51.00 – New 31.4 6.8 44.88 ∗∗∗ < 0.001 <0.001 Random 52.2 10.8 50.88 – Moved 45.7 9.0 51.13 0.885 5.4.5 Why Might Fork Resolution Fail to Improve Accuracy? As shown earlier, repeated flips rescue trajectories and generally recover more accuracy as more corrections are applied. Yet OPD can resolve more forks without improving accuracy, and some runs deteriorate. Fork resolution tracks changes in the preferred token, while generation depends on the full distribution. Entropy remains stable in the main Qwen pair, while late gains in the 8B-to-4B pair accompany declining entropy ( Figure 5 ). Even where argmax agreement is preserved, entropy rises and the shared token’s probability falls in Llama, Gemma, and IFEval ( Table 21 ). The student therefore becomes less likely to sample that token despite retaining it as its preferred choice. We suspect that training acquires useful corrections while destabilizing choices elsewhere. A good OPD training schedule should help the student resolve more valuable forks while maintaining entropy stability ( Section H.4 ). 6 Conclusion We systematically study OPD from the KD perspective by separating the value of teacher corrections from the student’s ability to learn them. Evaluating OPD requires measuring both the teacher preferences acquired during training and the accuracy still recoverable through controlled corrections. Repeated flips recover much more accuracy than a single intervention, and gains continue to accumulate as more corrections are applied. Mass- and KL-based selection allocate repeated corrections more effectively than random flips at counts of similar magnitude, despite weak prediction of individual single-flip gains. Across model pairs, fork resolution follows smoother trends than accuracy and often approaches a plateau. In the main pair, only 35.5% of original forks are resolved by the final checkpoint. Learnability patterns differ across teachers, and blind forks in the main pair are less often resolved despite their larger intervention gains. Intervention value therefore cannot substitute for evidence of learnability. Selective intervention remains useful after OPD, showing that saturated training accuracy need not mean that the teacher’s useful guidance has been exhausted. Greater token agreement nevertheless does not consistently produce better answers. Accuracy declines commonly accompany rising entropy across adjacent checkpoints. In several runs, entropy also rises where argmax agreement is preserved and the shared preferred token’s probability falls. These changes motivate our hypothesis that OPD acquires useful corrections while destabilizing choices elsewhere. One direction is selective OPD that downweights New and Moved forks and places more supervision on Unchanged forks. A curriculum could further organize these corrections by their value and learnability, adjusting the pace of supervision to acquire more useful teacher preferences while maintaining distributional stability. Limitations Our experiments cover several teacher–student pairs from the Qwen, Llama, and Gemma families, but the tasks are limited to mathematics and instruction following. Token-level interventions require a shared tokenizer within each pair. The single-flip study deliberately selects problems that the teacher solves and the student fails, making teacher corrections easier to observe. Its average gains therefore characterize this selected setting rather than randomly sampled problems. We separate OPD into two questions: which teacher corrections improve student trajectories, and how the student learns those corrections. Controlled interventions measure the value of corrections, while checkpoint analysis characterizes which teacher preferences are acquired on fixed pre-training prefixes. Determining what makes valuable corrections learnable remains a question for further work. Our proposed training directions still require evaluation of whether they improve learning while preserving distributional stability. References Agarwal et al. (2024) R. Agarwal, N. Vieillard, Y. Zhou, P. Stanczyk, S. Ramos Garea, M. Geist, and O. Bachem On-Policy Distillation of Language Models: Learning from Self-Generated Mistakes . In International Conference on Learning Representations (ICLR) , Note: arXiv:2306.13649 External Links: Link Cited by: §1 , §2 , §3 . Chen et al. (2023) C. Chen, S. Borgeaud, G. Irving, J. Lespiau, L. Sifre, and J. Jumper Accelerating Large Language Model Decoding with Speculative Sampling . arXiv preprint arXiv:2302.01318 . External Links: Link Cited by: §2 . Chen et al. (2025) Q. Chen, Z. Liu, P. Sun, S. Li, G. Wang, Z. Liu, Y. Wen, S. Feng, and T. Zhang ReSpec: Towards Optimizing Speculative Decoding in Reinforcement Learning Systems . arXiv preprint arXiv:2510.26475 . External Links: Link Cited by: §1 . Cover and Thomas (2006) T. M. Cover and J. A. Thomas Elements of Information Theory . 2 edition , Wiley . Cited by: §4.2 . DeepSeek-AI (2025) DeepSeek-AI DeepSeek-R1: Incentivizing Reasoning Capability in LLMs via Reinforcement Learning . arXiv preprint arXiv:2501.