uses a PyTorch generator seeded from the seed, batch index, and shard. Random-flip controls use a NumPy generator with seed 0. A.2 Population Statistics For Section 5.1 , we use 1,000 problems with 4 student rollouts each, temperature 1.0 1.0 , top- p p 1.0 1.0 , and at most 3,072 new tokens, following the rollout settings of Li et al. (2026) . The two next-token distributions at every position are scored with a top-16 truncation. A.3 Single Flip Each fork receives four sampled teacher segments of length L ∈ { 1 , 16 , 128 , ∞ } L\in\{1,16,128,\infty\} and four student continuations per finite segment. Success is the fraction of continuations ending correct. The 16 independent student resamples per fork yield 94,816 baseline continuations. At L = 1 L=1 , we retain only teacher paths changing the original student token. All four teacher samples repeat that token at 25 positions, leaving 5,901 eligible forks. At each of the 5,926 forks in Section 5.2 , the teacher samples 4 continuations, temperature 0.7 0.7 , top- p p 0.95 0.95 , at most 8,192 tokens in an 8,192-token context. The L ∈ { 1 , 16 , 128 } L\in\{1,16,128\} segments are prefixes of these, and the student writes 4 continuations of at most 3,072 tokens after each. For the reported L = 1 L=1 results, we rerun student continuations on NVIDIA L40S GPUs, reusing the same four teacher replacement tokens per fork and drawing four continuations per token. The resample arm also runs on L40S, drawing 16 student continuations directly from each fork prefix. Both arms use the same vLLM pipeline, temperature 0.7 0.7 , top- p p 0.95 0.95 , a 3,072-token generation cap, and a 12,288-token context limit, and are paired by position. Longer-segment outcomes and token-level diagnostics retain the original A100 runs. A.4 Dual-Track Decoding For Section 5.3 , both models decode in lockstep in transformers, temperature 0.7 0.7 , top- p p 0.95 0.95 , at most 8,192 tokens, with the answer-zone freeze of Section 4.3 . The student-alone and teacher-alone rows of Table 12 are 4 vLLM samples per problem with the same settings and the same cap. The teacher pass@4 behind Table 14 uses the 8,192-token cap; an earlier 4,096-token run truncated 38% of the teacher’s answers and was discarded. Synchronous decoding uses KV caches and batch size 6. Before each replacement decision, we check the last 12 generated tokens for the answer-zone freeze. Each trajectory records token authorship, the student’s sampled token, both argmaxes, and A A and B B . The additional model-pair and dataset experiments use chunked vLLM decoding. The student generates a chunk of tokens, which the teacher scores under the same prefixes. We accept tokens before the first selected fork, replace its token, discard the remaining chunk, and resume from the corrected prefix. Chunk size controls how many student tokens are generated and scored per round, not the length of teacher intervention. These experiments use chunks of 32 tokens, except for the 4B-to-0.6B KL runs, which use 8. A.4.1 Reflection-Word List We use Relay-OPD’s published list of wait, but, hmm, actually, hold, however, yet, oh, alternatively, no, ah, oops, well . Matching ignores case, leading whitespace, and attached punctuation, yielding 79 Qwen3 token ids. Split forms such as Wait followed by punctuation still match the word token. We retain the full list, including words that also have non-reflective uses in mathematics, such as “no solution” and “well-defined”. A.4.2 Additional Model Pairs We also use Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 to Qwen3-0.6B and Qwen3-8B to Qwen3-4B-Base on the same 200 problems, with four trajectories per problem, temperature 0.7, top- p p 0.95, an 8,192-token generation cap, and a 12,288-token context limit. Teacher replacements use argmax and stop at the answer-zone boundary. Reverse KL uses the student’s top-16 tokens plus a residual-probability bucket. Each pair has five mass thresholds, five KL thresholds, and seven random probabilities, all with 800 completed trajectories. A.4.3 IFEval We use Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 as teacher and Qwen3-1.7B as student on all 541 official IFEval prompts, with four trajectories per prompt. Standalone baselines and interventions use temperature 0.7, top- p p 0.95, a 6,144-token generation cap, and an 8,192-token context limit, with thinking disabled. We report official strict prompt accuracy, requiring all instructions in a prompt to be satisfied. Interventions use teacher argmax replacements and no answer-zone freeze. Each of the five mass thresholds, five KL thresholds, and seven random probabilities has 2,164 completed trajectories. Fork coverage divides total flips by total argmax disagreements on each policy’s generated trajectories. A.4.4 Llama Model Pair We use Llama-3.2-3B-Instruct as teacher and Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct as student on the same 200 DAPO-Math problems. Each of five mass thresholds, five KL thresholds, and seven random probabilities has 800 completed trajectories. Sampling uses temperature 0.7 and top- p p 0.95, with an 8,192-token generation cap and a 12,288-token context limit. Interventions use teacher argmax replacements and the answer-zone freeze. Reverse KL uses the student’s top-16 tokens plus a residual-probability bucket. A.4.5 Gemma Model Pair We also evaluate Gemma-3-12B-it as teacher and Gemma-3-4B-it as student on the same 200 DAPO-Math problems, with four trajectories per problem. Sampling uses temperature 0.7 and top- p p 0.95, with an 8,192-token generation cap and a 12,288-token context limit. We compare mass-based, KL-based, and random fork selection using teacher argmax replacements and the answer-zone freeze. Reverse KL uses the student’s top-16 tokens plus a residual-probability bucket. A.5 Handoff For each of the 800 dual-track trajectories, we retain the prefix up to a fraction f ∈ { 0.25 , 0.5 , 0.75 , 0.9 } f\in\{0.25,0.5,0.75,0.9\} of its length before the answer zone and sample two student continuations. We also include f = 1 f=1 , where the student writes only the answer line, and the student-alone baseline at f = 0 f=0 . For Section G.2 , the student continues each cut trajectory in vLLM, with 2 continuations per cut, temperature 0.7 0.7 , top- p p 0.95 0.95 , and at most 8,192 new tokens within a 12,288-token context. A.6 After OPD We train Qwen3-1.7B with vanilla OPD using Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507 as teacher. The DAPO-Math training data exclude the 1,000 evaluation problems. The current analysis uses checkpoints at steps 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 48, 64, 128, 192, and the final step 256. A.6.1 Fork Resolution and Accuracy Each checkpoint scores all 5,237,483 positions from the same 4,000 pre-training student trajectories. Position identifiers align checkpoint scores with the saved pre-training student and teacher preferences. All 729,574 original argmax forks are included, even if the sampled student token equals the teacher’s argmax. Solved, Unchanged, and Moved partition this set using the definitions in Section 5.4 . New forks lie outside the original set. Accuracy uses each checkpoint’s own 4,000 rollouts on the evaluation problems, with four samples per problem, temperature 1.0, top- p p 1.0, and an 8,192-token generation cap. The pre-training accuracy uses the same settings. Unlike accuracy, fork changes are measured on fixed pre-training prefixes. A.6.2 Fork Resolution by Pre-Training Scores We group the original forks using their saved pre-training scores. Teacher mass μ t T \mu_{t}^{T} is the teacher’s probability of the base student’s argmax, and reverse KL uses the full vocabulary on the same prefix. Group membership is fixed across checkpoints. Resolution rates divide the number resolved at each checkpoint by the original count within that group. These are pooled position-level rates, not problem-weighted estimates. A.6.3 Teacher Intervention after OPD We repeat dual-track decoding at steps 16, 64, and the final checkpoint on the same 200-problem subset, using four trajectories per problem at temperature 0.7 and top- p p 0.95, with an 8,192-token cap. The mass thresholds are 10 − 4 10^{-4} , 10 − 3 10^{-3} , 10 − 2 10^{-2} , and 0.1 0.1 . Random-flip probabilities are 0.06 0.06 , 0.11 0.11 , 0.46 0.46 , and 0.90 0.90 , supplemented by the approximately 100-flip random controls described below. Standalone checkpoint baselines use the same sampling settings. Figure 4 includes only complete 800-trajectory runs. The additional Final-checkpoint KL interventions in Section H.2 use the student’s top-16 tokens plus a residual-probability bucket. The 0.6B student uses the same 200 mathematics problems and decoding limits. IFEval uses all 541 official prompts, with a 6,144-token generation cap and an 8,192-token context limit. Both settings use temperature 0.7 and top- p p 0.95, with four trajectories per problem or prompt. A.6.4 Online Fork-Group Selection The pre-training student, trained student, and teacher score each current prefix to determine its fork group online. This differs from the fixed-prefix composition analysis. These interventions use the same checkpoints and sampling settings as Section A.6.3 . Old, Unchanged, and Random policies use probabilities calibrated toward 100 flips per trajectory. New and Moved policies intervene at every eligible fork, yielding fewer flips. At step 16, New is compared with random probability 0.06 and Moved with 0.11. At steps 64 and 256, both are compared with the approximately 100-flip random control. Step-64 New and Moved runs combine the initial shard with three further shards using the same probability of one, for 800 distinct problem–sample pairs per policy. All realized counts are reported in Table 17 . A.6.5 Additional Checkpoint Evaluations We evaluate the additional Qwen, Llama, and Gemma checkpoints on 1,000 DAPO-Math problems with four rollouts per problem. The IFEval checkpoints use the 541 official prompts with four rollouts per prompt. These evaluations use temperature 0.7 and top- p p 0.95, with response caps of 8,192 tokens on DAPO-Math and 6,144 on IFEval. The supplementary 1.7B checkpoint curve also uses these sampling settings, separately from the temperature-1.0 evaluation in Table 16 . Fork changes use each pair’s own fixed pre-training trajectories and original fork count. We do not pool positions across pairs. Mass and reverse-KL groups retain their pre-training membership at every checkpoint. Fixed-prefix fork statistics are complete at all ten checkpoints for every pair. The additional Gemma and Llama fork-group interventions use 200 DAPO-Math problems with one rollout per problem at steps 16, 64, and 256. Each policy intervenes at every eligible fork in its selected group, at temperature 0.7 and top- p p 0.95. The response cap is 8,192 tokens and the context cap is 12,288. Intervention stops at the configured answer marker. Resolved positions already share the teacher’s argmax and are not eligible for an argmax flip. These runs report the realized counts without budget calibration. Student-alone controls average four rollouts per problem on the same 200 problems at the same temperature and top- p p . Llama uses the corresponding no-intervention runs from the same decoder. Gemma uses the 200-problem subset of its checkpoint rollouts, with a 9,216-token context cap. We verify that every selected prompt permits the full 8,192-token response cap. The IFEval fork-group interventions use the same 541 prompts and four trajectories per prompt at steps 16, 64, and 256. Their no-intervention controls use the same sampling settings. Group policies are calibrated using recorded teacher insertions. We verify that each recorded insertion changes the student’s preferred token in these completed runs, so the reported counts are actual flips. For the entropy analysis in Section H.4 , we score each pair’s fixed pre-training trajectories with the base student and every trained checkpoint. We compute full-vocabulary entropy from untempered model probabilities and average over token positions. DAPO-Math includes 4,000 trajectories per pair and IFEval includes 2,164. Scoring stops at the first complete answer when an answer boundary is found, excluding subsequent repetition. Accuracy uses each checkpoint’s own rollouts at temperature 0.7 and top- p p 0.95. Appendix B Statistical Tests The tests distinguish whether teacher intervention helps from whether one group or selection rule helps more than its comparator. Positions within a trajectory and trajectories from the same problem can be correlated, so we aggregate differences before testing rather than treating every fork as an independent observation. All reported t t -tests are two-sided. Group-comparison p p -values are unadjusted for multiple testing and interpreted as exploratory. Asterisks refer to the comparison specified in each table. B.1 Single-Flip Tests We ask whether a teacher replacement improves recovery beyond another student attempt from the same prefix. At each fork, we subtract the success rate of 16 independent student resamples from the teacher-intervention success rate. Comparing continuations from the same prefix controls for how recoverable that position already is without the teacher. At L = 1 L=1 , only teacher paths changing the original token contribute, leaving 5,901 of 5,926 positions eligible. All teacher paths contribute at longer lengths. Trajectory-Level Gain ( p T p_{T} ). To estimate the average gain across failed trajectories, we average paired position gains within each trajectory and apply a one-sample t t -test against zero to the 40 trajectory means ( p T p_{T} ). Each trajectory receives equal weight. Reported single-flip confidence intervals use the Student- t t distribution. Problem-Level Gain ( p P p_{P} ). To assess the gain across problems while aggregating their correlated trajectories, we pool eligible position gains within each problem and test the 10 problem means against zero ( p P p_{P} ). Each problem receives equal weight, so this test changes both the unit of analysis and the weighting relative to p T p_{T} . Bootstrap Check. We use 2,000 trajectory-bootstrap replicates to check the sensitivity of the overall gain interval to the Student- t t construction. Each replicate resamples whole trajectory means rather than individual forks. Fork-Group Differences. A positive gain over resampling does not establish that a group is more useful than other forks. We therefore compare the group’s mean paired gain with the mean paired gain at the remaining positions. Within each trajectory or problem, we subtract the latter from the former and test these contrasts against zero. Each included unit must contain at least three eligible positions on each side. A positive significant contrast indicates a larger average teacher-intervention gain in that group, rather than merely a higher absolute recovery rate. Full estimates and tests, including nonsignificant comparisons, appear in Sections D.2 and D.3 . Predicting the benefit of an individual flip is a separate question, addressed in Section D.4 . B.2 Dual-Track Tests Each policy generates four trajectories on the same 200 randomly sampled problems. For each problem, we subtract the comparator’s mean correctness from the tested policy’s mean correctness, then apply a one-sample t t -test against zero to the 200 differences. This is equivalent to a paired t t -test on the two sets of problem means. Pairing is over problems, not individual trajectories, because the policies generate different continuations. Fork Selection versus Random Flips. This comparison asks whether choosing forks by a feature improves accuracy over random intervention. We apply the problem-paired test above to the selected policy and its random control. Controls approximate intervention coverage or counts, as specified for each experiment, rather than matching the exact realized number of flips. Random-control settings and comparison results for the original model pair are reported in Section G.1 . Teacher Sampling versus Argmax. This comparison asks whether sampling a teacher token changes accuracy relative to taking the teacher’s argmax under the same selection rule. We apply the same problem-paired test to the two complete policies ( Table 15 ). Fork-Group Selection after OPD. This comparison asks whether a fork’s training history identifies useful intervention targets. We compare each group-selection policy with its designated random control using the same problem-paired test. Control choices and realized counts are specified in Sections A.6.4 and H.3 . Wilcoxon Check. For the original random-control comparisons, we also apply a Wilcoxon signed-rank test to the problem-level differences. This checks sensitivity to the paired t t -test’s treatment of those differences and gives the same significance conclusions. Welch Check. For the same comparisons, a Welch t t -test compares the 800 trajectory outcomes from each policy without pairing individual trajectories. This alternative aggregation also gives the same significance conclusions, while the problem-paired test remains the reported comparison. B.3 Handoff Tests Constant-Gain Residuals. This test asks whether accuracy at each teacher exit point departs from a constant-gain-per-flip model. Predicted accuracy at exit fraction f f interpolates between the problem’s student-alone rate and the trajectory’s accuracy at f = 1 f=1 , weighted by the fraction of flips made before f f . We subtract this prediction from observed recovery and test the mean residual against zero over the 800 trajectories, then repeat the test after averaging within each problem. Exit-Position Trend. This test asks whether departures from the constant-gain model vary systematically with the teacher’s exit position. We fit a residual slope against f f within each trajectory and test the mean slope against zero. Results for both handoff tests appear in Section G.2 . Appendix C Population Statistics Figure 6: Reverse KL